diff --git a/accel/henge/engine/VENDORED-FROM b/accel/henge/engine/VENDORED-FROM
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000..e40cb19a34
--- /dev/null
+++ b/accel/henge/engine/VENDORED-FROM
@@ -0,0 +1,171 @@
+Henge engine -- vendored copy.  DO NOT EDIT IN PLACE.
+
+Upstream
+    repo     /data/用户/项目/qemu-acce/src/WindowsiOS
+    commit   0071d69  (2026-11-11, "feat(ios): add 1-tap Microsoft Paint launcher")
+    licence  see LICENSE + THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md at the upstream root
+
+Vendored at
+    QEMU     v11.1.3  (005c61c0ef)
+    date     2026-11-15
+
+What is here, and where it came from
+    core/*.c core/*.h     upstream core/                     -- unchanged
+    compat/*              port-android/compat/              -- unchanged
+    host/jit_region.h     upstream host/jit_region.h        -- unchanged
+    host/jit_region_linux.c
+                          port-android/jit_region_linux.c   -- unchanged
+    host/enter_elf.S      upstream host/enter.S, sed'd:
+                          s/\b_hv_/hv_/g                     -- GENERATED
+
+Why a copy and not a symlink into the reference tree
+    The tree under src/WindowsiOS is the only known-good reference for this
+    engine (nine rounds of measurements were made against it).  Bridging it to
+    QEMU needs changes that must NOT land in that tree:
+
+      * devices/ is not vendored at all: QEMU owns the device models, so the
+        engine's gicv3/timer/pl011 are replaced by QEMU's, and the engine gets
+        its interrupts from QEMU instead of from its own GIC.  That is a
+        behavioural change to the engine's machine layer.
+      * RAM arrives from a MemoryListener rather than from a board file, and
+        can be *removed* (hv_machine_del_ram did not exist upstream).
+      * hv_phys_read/hv_phys_write need a catch-all fallback to QEMU's
+        address_space_rw for MMIO.
+
+    Keeping those in accel/henge/ (outside engine/) is the plan; anything that
+    has to be edited inside engine/ is a sync conflict and must be upstreamed
+    or recorded in LOCAL-EDITS below.
+
+LOCAL-EDITS
+    local-edits.patch -- applied on top of the vendored copy, eight files:
+
+      core/cpu.h        two exit reasons appended (SYSREG_EXT=10, PSCI_EXT=11)
+      core/machine.h    two seam flags (sysreg_external, psci_external), the
+                        timer fields (timer_freq / scale / epoch), and feat_lse
+      core/sysreg.h     hv_guest_ticks() for the host's clock calibration
+      core/sysreg.c     the seam itself: unmodelled MRS/MSR, the ICC_* hooks,
+                        the ID group (op0=3, CRn=0) and the timer control
+                        registers leave the block instead of being answered
+                        from the engine's own fixed model
+      core/exception.c  SMC/HVC seam, same idea for PSCI
+      core/dispatch.c   WFI path accounting in wait_for_interrupt(), off unless
+                        HV_WFI_COUNT is set.  Answers one question: does WFI
+                        return because the line is already high (A, the guest
+                        keeps running inside the engine, invisible from
+                        outside) or because there is no tick (B, the whole run
+                        exits and QEMU parks the thread)?  Under henge it is
+                        B almost every time -- A was measured at 4 and 2 over a
+                        55 s two-vCPU boot -- which is why a vCPU idling at
+                        full guest speed cannot be the SMP stall's cause.
+      core/machine.c    (a) feat_lse initialised to 1 in init_rest(), so the
+                        standalone engine keeps executing ARMv8.1 atomics
+                        exactly as it always has.  The gate that would lower it
+                        lives in the accelerator and is currently forced to
+                        "on": see the comment there, it regressed the Linux
+                        baseline when driven by cpu_isar_feature().
+                        (b) hv_phys_write(): a read-only window's stores fall
+                        through to the MMIO table instead of being discarded.
+                        Dropping the write and forwarding it are both "no store
+                        into RAM", and only forwarding lets a device's command
+                        state machine see it.  This is the write half of romd.
+                        (c) hv_machine_add_ram(): reuse a retired slot before
+                        growing the table.  hv_machine_del_ram() retires in
+                        place and does not compact (see the note further down
+                        on why compaction is a SIGSEGV), so without reuse every
+                        add/remove cycle burned one of HV_MAX_RAM's 8 slots.
+                        pflash does exactly that twice per CFI command write,
+                        which is how UEFI ended up booting with its variable
+                        store window missing from the engine.
+      core/translate.c  LSE gate, active: when feat_lse is clear, HV_MEM_ATOMIC
+                        (LDADD/LDCLR/LDEOR/LDSET/LDSMAX/LDSMIN/LDUMAX/LDUMIN/
+                        SWP) translates to UNDEF.
+                        The predicate is narrow on purpose.  An earlier version
+                        also listed HV_MEM_CAS and HV_MEM_CASP, which
+                        decode_exclusive() produces for the pair-exclusive
+                        forms, so it rejected STXR/STXP -- ARMv8.0 baseline
+                        instructions -- on a v8.0 CPU model.  The kernel stopped
+                        booting entirely.  Measured afterwards with
+                        work/probe/llsc.S: A (LDXR/STXR) and B (LDXP/STXP) pass,
+                        C (LDADD) is rejected, and the Linux baseline reaches
+                        userspace in 18.36 s with the gate on.
+                        Driven by HV_NO_LSE=1 in the accelerator; without it
+                        feat_lse stays 1 and nothing is gated.
+
+    romd (virt.flash0/flash1) IS mirrored, and the semantics matter.
+      -QEMU's pflash regions report is_ram=0, is_rom=0, is_romd=1, so a
+      `is_ram || is_rom` filter skips them and edk2 dies at pc=0 with
+      HV_EXIT_FAULT_LOOP on the first fetch.
+      They are mirrored READ-ONLY with hv_phys_write() forwarding a read-only
+      window's stores to the MMIO table instead of dropping them.  That is what
+      romd means: direct reads, device writes.  Registering them writable
+      corrupts the flash image; registering them "read-only RAM" that discards
+      writes stalls the CFI command state machine.  Both were tried and both
+      break something; the split is the one that works.
+      Measured together: -kernel boots to userspace (END 19.03) AND edk2 gets
+      past DxeCore to BdsDxe.
+
+
+    UEFI: PORTED.  edk2 boots to the EFI shell (measured: erase errors 0,
+      assertions 0, "UEFI Interactive Shell v2.2" + "Shell>" at -smp 1 and 2).
+
+      The last mile was not in the engine at all, and is recorded here because
+      both bugs were invisible from the engine's side and both are one-liners
+      in the accelerator:
+
+      1. The MemoryListener was never assigned.  `henge_memory_listener` was
+         defined with region_add/region_del and then registered as
+         `memory_listener_register(&h->listener, ...)` without ever copying the
+         struct into it -- so both callbacks were NULL.  QEMU dispatches with
+         `if (_listener->_callback)`, so the listener sat in the queue and was
+         skipped in silence.  setup_post walks the finished flatview directly,
+         which hides this completely at boot; the hole only opens afterwards.
+         What opens it is pflash: every CFI command-sequence write calls
+         memory_region_rom_device_set_romd(), which flips ->romd_mode, which
+         changes the FlatRange, which is the ONLY signal that the mirror must
+         go away.  With no callback the flash window stayed mirrored through
+         romd-off, so the guest read the variable store out of RAM instead of
+         the CFI state machine, read 0xFFFFFFFF (erased flash content) as the
+         status register, and looped forever unlocking a block it could never
+         unlock -- 101 writes from a single PC, then 9.8M once traced.  UEFI
+         then had no Variable Arch Protocol, so Variable, VariableWrite,
+         Capsule, MonotonicCounter, RTC and Bds were all "not present" and
+         DxeCore asserted at MdeModulePkg/Core/Dxe/DxeMain/DxeMain.c(567).
+         Note for the next reader: that ASSERT is downstream of the flash
+         problem, not a second bug.
+         Diagnosed by tracing at three layers at once -- QEMU's pflash trace
+         (which values reached the device), a new trace=mmio channel in the
+         accelerator (which address/width/value/PC the engine forwarded, and
+         the value read back), and a temporary print of
+         flatrange_equal()'s verdict.  Each was necessary: the pflash trace
+         ruled out value corruption, the PC ruled out a guest-side retry loop,
+         and "equal=0 but region_del never fired" is what pointed at the
+         listener.
+
+      2. hv_machine_add_ram() leaked a slot per add/remove cycle.
+         hv_machine_del_ram() retired the entry in place (host = NULL,
+         size = 0) and left m->nram alone, so add_ram -- which always did
+         `&m->ram[m->nram++]` -- consumed a permanent slot.  HV_MAX_RAM is 8,
+         and with the listener live each romd toggle is a del + add of the
+         whole 64 MB window, twice per CFI command write.  UEFI therefore
+         booted with twenty-seven "failed to mirror virt.flash1" lines behind
+         it: past the eighth toggle the window was simply absent from the
+         engine and every flash access silently became an MMIO round trip.
+         Fixed by reusing a retired slot in add_ram.  Compacting the array in
+         del_ram instead is a SIGSEGV: hv_ram_lookup() hands out HvRam * that
+         callers hold across further add/remove calls, so moving entries turns
+         those into use-after-move.  That was tried first and crashed at the
+         first romd toggle; the comment in add_ram says so.
+
+    All of it is additive and inactive by default (every flag is 0, every scale
+    is 1/1), so the standalone build behaves exactly as before -- that is the
+    point of flags over #ifdefs: one code path, two hosts.
+
+    The patch is not optional.  sync-from-upstream.sh applies it to the fresh
+    copy and fails loudly if it no longer applies: a refresh that silently lost
+    these edits would still compile and then behave as a plain host-less engine.
+        ./sync-from-upstream.sh            # copy + apply
+        ./sync-from-upstream.sh --check    # verify, exit 1 on drift
+
+How to refresh
+    ./sync-from-upstream.sh            # copies and reports any drift
+    ./sync-from-upstream.sh --check    # diff only, no writes
